# vesma-mcp-front platform env slot — template.
#
# Copy to the env.d slot of the target plane, make it 0600:
#
#   user:   cp mcp-front.env.example ~/.vesma/env.d/mcp-front.env
#           chmod 600 ~/.vesma/env.d/mcp-front.env
#   system: sudo cp mcp-front.env.example /etc/vesma/env.d/mcp-front.env
#           sudo chmod 600 /etc/vesma/env.d/mcp-front.env
#           sudo chown root:vesma /etc/vesma/env.d/mcp-front.env   # if root-installed
#
# Fail-closed: the supervisor refuses the front start unless this file
# exists with EXACTLY mode 0600, owned by the supervisor user, and
# OUTSIDE the manifests directory (layout §3.5, LY-03).
#
# C2 (ADR-0050): the front carries HARNESS Authorization headers THROUGH,
# unread and unstored — it validates no harness identity. What it MAY
# hold since ADR-0051 (W4) is ITS OWN service identity (see
# VESMA_FRONT_SERVICE_TOKEN below) — the credential the replay leg uses
# when it delivers spooled writes after the core recovers. That one
# value is the only secret that ever belongs in this file. Never commit
# a filled copy of this file.
#
# The core MCP plane this front forwards to (loopback only — the module
# refuses any non-loopback target, because this URL is where harness
# tokens GO; C2). Default instance core = 127.0.0.1:8787, plane = /mcp/.
VESMA_FRONT_TARGET=http://127.0.0.1:8787/mcp/

# ── Degraded write mode (ADR-0051, W4) ──────────────────────────────
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: without VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_MAX_DURATION the front runs
# REFUSE-WRITES — an unreachable core answers the honest fixed 503 and
# NOTHING is spooled (the loud boot line says so). Setting the TTL below
# is the explicit opt-in to degraded mode.
#
# The degraded-mode TTL (mandatory to enable; the documented
# recommendation is 7d). Systemd-style grammar: 7d / 12h / 30m / 600s,
# or plain seconds.
#VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_MAX_DURATION=7d
#
# Probe cadence while degraded, and its backoff ceiling (30s / 10m).
#VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_POLL_INTERVAL=30s
#VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_BACKOFF_MAX=600s
#
# Failed probes before HEALTHY flips to DEGRADED (default 3).
#VESMA_FRONT_PROBE_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=3
#
# Spool byte quota — exhaustion blocks writes (BLOCKED_WRITES) until a
# replay drains the spool. Default 1073741824 (1 GiB).
#VESMA_FRONT_SPOOL_MAX_BYTES=1073741824
#
# The repeat cadence of the degraded log line (default 15m).
#VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_SIGNAGE_INTERVAL=15m
#
# OPTIONAL out-of-machine escalation webhook (best-effort POST, 5s
# timeout): fires on entering DEGRADED and on the 80% TTL crossing.
#VESMA_FRONT_DEGRADED_NOTIFY_URL=
#
# OPTIONAL explicit spool instance label (default: the instance-id file
# at the default store home, else "default").
#VESMA_FRONT_INSTANCE=
#
# THE FRONT'S OWN SERVICE TOKEN — the agent token the REPLAY leg
# authenticates with when it delivers spooled operations to the core
# (issue: vesma agent-token issue --agent <front-service> --node
# <instance-id>; keep the TTL short and rotate it). The front NEVER
# stores harness tokens (C2) — this is its own identity, and the ONLY
# secret in this file. Without it, spooling works but REPLAY refuses
# loudly (nothing is applied until the token is configured).
#VESMA_FRONT_SERVICE_TOKEN=
